
########## dimensions.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

       11  [P] from the preprint
       15  [V] verified against a named source
        6  [A] assumed range or choice

1. Is 224 in the Cayley-Dickson tower?

      the tower doubles at every step: [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512]
      sedenions, the algebra the paper names: dimension 16   in the tower: True

        12 in the tower: False
       112 in the tower: False
       224 in the tower: False
       448 in the tower: False

      224 = 2 x 112 = 224, and 112 is itself not in the tower.

      So the doubling the paper invokes is not Cayley-Dickson doubling.
      Cayley-Dickson doubling takes an algebra of dimension n to one of
      dimension 2n starting from 1, which gives the powers of two and
      nothing else; 112 and 224 are not among them.  Multiplying an
      arbitrary number by two is a different operation that happens to
      share the word.

      And the sedenions are 16-dimensional, so "Sedenion architecture
      (which is the mathematical foundation of the -224D space)" does not
      connect: nothing in the sedenions has 224 of anything.  The algebra
      is real, its zero divisors are real (zerodivisors.py Sec. 1-2), and
      the dimension it lives in is 16.

      Where 112 came from was already measured in openql-eval: the openQL
      v1.0 sentence that derived it was deleted in v1.1 without being
      replaced, so 224 is a doubling of a number the series no longer
      offers a derivation for.

2. The multiple-of-16 claim, which is correct

      [V] WMMA tile granularity: 16 x 16

        dim      /16   exact    tiles   MMA ops per node   occupancy
         12    0.750   False    1x1                    1     56.25%
        112    7.000    True    7x7                   49    100.00%
        224   14.000    True   14x14                 196    100.00%

      224 / 16 = 14 exactly, so Sec. 4 is right about
      the alignment and right that it is not a hardware burden in the
      sense of wasted lanes.  The 100 % saturation claim is also correct
      as an occupancy statement.

      Two things the section does not say.  112 / 16 = 7 is exact too, so
      the alignment is inherited rather than gained -- doubling a multiple
      of 16 stays a multiple of 16, for any such number.  And a 224D node
      is 196 MMA operations, not one: the tiles are the unit the
      hardware executes, and there are 196 of them per node.

      The 12D layer is the one that never tiled: 12 / 16 = 0.75, so a
      12x12 node fills 56.25 % of a single tile.  That layer is carried
      unchanged from openQL and the alignment argument does not cover it.

3. "Negative dimensions"

      [P] Sec. 2 divides space into "positive (matter) and negative
          (vacuum/antimatter) intensional nodes" at +/-12D, +/-112D, +/-224D

      Dimension is a cardinality: the number of coordinates needed to
      specify a point.  There is no vector space of dimension -224 over any
      field, because there is no set with -224 elements in a basis.

      What the paper describes -- a mirror copy of the state space holding
      the antiparticle sector -- is a perfectly ordinary construction, and
      it is MORE dimensions, not negative ones:

           layer   positive   mirror   total state
           +/-12         12       12            24
          +/-112        112      112           224
          +/-224        224      224           448
       all three        348      348           696

      So the engine's largest layer is a 448-dimensional state, which is
      what the hardware has to hold and move.  Calling half of it negative
      is a labelling of the two halves, not a property of the space, and
      the costs in hardware.py are computed on the real figure.

4. The dimensions the cited physics actually uses

      [P] Sec. 2: "The positive 224D contains complete string theory and
          supersymmetry (all possible vibrations of matter)."

      [V] critical dimension, superstring theory        10
      [V] critical dimension, bosonic string theory     26
      [V] M-theory                                      11
      [P] the layer said to contain it                 224

          224 == 10:  False
          224 == 26:  False
          224 == 11:  False

      The critical dimensions are not free parameters; they are fixed by
      anomaly cancellation.  224 is not one of them, and the section gives
      no embedding, compactification or other relation between the two
      numbers.  This is recorded as an unsupported claim rather than a
      measured defect: there is no construction here to check.

########## zerodivisors.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

       11  [P] from the preprint
       15  [V] verified against a named source
        6  [A] assumed range or choice

1. The Cayley-Dickson tower, built here and checked

      dimensions in the tower: [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256]

        dim   commutative   associative   alternative   |xy| = |x||y|
          1          True          True          True       0.00e+00
          2          True          True          True       1.50e-16
          4         False          True          True       0.00e+00
          8         False         False          True       1.41e-16
         16         False         False         False       1.10e-02

      Norm multiplicativity holds up to dimension 8 and
      fails at 16, which is exactly why zero divisors become possible there.
      That is Moreno's result, and the preprint's reference [3] is the right
      source for it: apt = True.

2. A real sedenion zero divisor, found by search

      Searching pairs e_i +/- e_j for a product of zero:
      pairs found: 168
      example:  (e1 + e10) x (e4 - e15) = 0
        |a| = 1.4142,  |b| = 1.4142,  |a x b| = 0.00e+00

      So the algebra claim of Sec. 3 is CORRECT.  Sedenions do have zero
      divisors, they are constructible, and the paper's citation for them is
      the right one.  This is the strongest part of the deposit and it is
      confirmed here from first principles rather than taken on trust.

3. But the engine does not need sedenions for this

      The preprint's own equation is about MATRICES: "there exist non-zero
      matrices A (positron) and B (electron)".  Zero divisors are ordinary
      in any matrix algebra over a field.  The smallest example:
        A = [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0]]   non-zero: True
        B = [[0.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]]   non-zero: True
        A @ B = [[0.0, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0]]   zero: True

      non-zero 224x224 real pairs with A @ B = 0, out of 200 trials: 200

      Any two singular matrices whose image and kernel line up multiply to
      zero.  No exotic algebra is involved, and the property is available
      at every dimension, not only at 224.  So the mechanism Sec. 3 reaches
      for sedenions to obtain is one the hardware already has for free.

4. The product cannot carry the energy away

      ||A||_F     =      34.1038
      ||B||_F     =     221.8755
      ||A @ B||_F =   4.0555e-13

      The product retains nothing of either operand's magnitude -- that is
      what being zero means.  So "the release of an absolute tensor vector
      into the energy register" cannot come from the product: there is no
      quantity left in it to release.

      To emit E = mc^2 the engine has to read ||A|| and ||B|| BEFORE the
      multiply, keep them somewhere, and then notice that the matter
      register went to zero in order to know to add them to the energy
      register.  Noticing that is a comparison against zero, i.e. exactly
      the "if (collision)" that Sec. 3 says is not needed.  The branch is
      not removed; it is moved from before the multiply to after it.

      And conservation runs the other way too: a zero product is reached by
      any aligned singular pair, so an engine that emits energy whenever a
      register hits zero emits it for every such pair, whether or not the
      two operands were a particle and its antiparticle.

5. The choice the architecture does not make

      matrix multiplication is associative: True
      sedenion multiplication is not (measured in Sec. 1 above)

      The architecture needs WMMA, which is matrix multiplication, and
      matrix multiplication is associative over any ring.  The mechanism
      needs the sedenions, which are not.  Both cannot hold at once, and
      the preprint asserts them in consecutive sections: Sec. 3 says the
      zero divisors come from sedenion mathematics, Sec. 4 says the node is
      an array of 14 x 14 WMMA blocks.

      This is the same structural point openql-eval measured for the
      octonions, where the span of the associators left 1 of 8 dimensions
      surviving any matrix form.  The sedenions are a strictly larger
      problem: they are not even alternative, as Sec. 1 shows.

########## hardware.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

       11  [P] from the preprint
       15  [V] verified against a named source
        6  [A] assumed range or choice

      hardware: RTX 3090, 142 TFLOP/s fp16 tensor, 936 GB/s, 1.70 GHz, 24 GiB

1. "a single processor clock cycle"

      whole-GPU fp16 throughput              83,529 FLOP/cycle

            node   FLOP per product   cycles of the WHOLE GPU
           12x12              3,456                     0.04
         112x112          2,809,856                    33.64
         224x224         22,478,848                   269.11

      So one annihilation at 224D is 269 cycles of the entire card,
      not one, and 8x worse than the 112D case openQL made the
      same claim for -- which is what cubing the dimension does.

      The claim is also of a kind the hardware cannot satisfy in principle:
      a WMMA instruction has a latency of several cycles, a 224D node is
      196 of them, and the operands have to be fetched first, which is the
      next section.

2. What a +/-224D node costs to hold

           layer     one node    with mirror   pairs in VRAM
           +/-12       0.3 KiB         0.6 KiB      44,739,243
          +/-112      24.5 KiB        49.0 KiB         513,588
          +/-224      98.0 KiB       196.0 KiB         128,397

      At the +/-224D layer the card holds 128,397 node pairs, a cube
      50 nodes on a side, state only -- nothing left for the rule
      matrices, the ray state or an output image.

      Against Sec. 5's tokamak application, at one node per particle:
        nodes the card holds                          128,397
        [A] particles in 1 mm^3 at 1e20 m^-3   100,000,000,000
        shortfall                                     778,834x   (5.9 orders)

      So the grid is about six orders of magnitude short of a single cubic
      millimetre of plasma.  The density is an order-of-magnitude figure and
      carries nothing here beyond the order of the shortfall.

3. The step rate the bandwidth allows, for a fixed grid

      A table over a card filled to capacity would be degenerate: filling
      the card and sweeping it once is about two VRAM passes whatever the
      node size, so every layer would return the same number by
      construction.  The grid is therefore held fixed and the layer varied,
      which is what makes the cost of the doubling visible.

      [A] grid 1,000,000 node pairs

           layer          state    bytes per sweep   sweeps/s  fits in VRAM
           +/-12        0.54 GiB            1.07 GiB     812.50          True
          +/-112       46.73 GiB           93.46 GiB       9.33         False
          +/-224      186.92 GiB          373.84 GiB       2.33         False

      112D -> 224D multiplies the traffic per sweep by 4x.

      A million-node grid does not fit on the card at either of the two
      upper layers.  For the largest grid that does fit at +/-224D:
        node pairs                                    128,397
        bytes per sweep                              48.00 GiB
        sweeps/s                                        18.16
      i.e. about 18 Hz for a 50-node cube.  Real time is 60.

4. The roofline, which the doubling does not move

           layer     state      matrix  overhead   W@s FLOP/B   W@S FLOP/B
           +/-12     24 B      288 B       12x          6.0          6.0
          +/-112    224 B   25,088 B      112x         56.0         56.0
          +/-224    448 B  100,352 B      224x        112.0        112.0

      The last two columns are equal at every layer, which is the result
      openql-eval measured for 112 and which survives the doubling: the
      matrix form sits at exactly the same point on the roofline as the
      state it encodes, while costing d times the traffic and d times the
      arithmetic.  Encoding d numbers as a d x d matrix buys no arithmetic
      intensity at any d.

      Which is the measured sense in which Sec. 4's "100% saturated" is
      true and not useful.  The tiles are full -- that is what 224/16 = 14
      exactly buys -- and the card is waiting on memory regardless.

5. What the doubling costs, relative to openQL

                      quantity           112D           224D    ratio
         node footprint, bytes         25,088        100,352       4x
        with the mirror, bytes         50,176        200,704       4x
              FLOP per product      2,809,856     22,478,848       8x
           WMMA tiles per node             49            196       4x

      So the step from openQL to openOmega is 4x the memory per node, 8x
      the arithmetic and 4x the tiles, before the mirror doubles the memory
      again.  Sec. 4 presents it as costless because the alignment is
      preserved; alignment is about waste inside a tile, not about how many
      tiles there are.
